WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.227
The first CTE remains at admission grain and adds previous discharge.

00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:09.612
The second CTE keeps only admissions with a previous encounter and calculates the gap.

00:00:09.612 --> 00:00:14.247
The final query joins patient names and applies the thirty-day classification.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:16.992
Run it and read the query stage by stage.

00:00:16.992 --> 00:00:20.282
Notice that the patient join is delayed until the final result because it is descriptive rather than needed

00:00:20.282 --> 00:00:28.007
for sequence logic. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.007 --> 00:00:31.757
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:33.337
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:40.567
In a real setting such as an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event,

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:46.102
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:47.362
before discussing syntax.

00:00:47.362 --> 00:00:52.560
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
